fact WorkItem(id: "WI-841", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 2 — SELECT: make the call-site bracket bind a REQUIREMENT SLOT. FOUR pieces (§4.2, §4.4, §4.5). (1) RULE (1)'s SORT-LEVEL SEEDING LEG — a call bracket must bind the ENCLOSING SORT's type params, which today it cannot: measured, `Box.mk[T = String](5)` on `sort Box { sort T = ? }` errors at the RETURN (`expected Box[T = String], got Box[T = Int64]` — T was inferred from the ARGUMENT and the binding contributed nothing), and `Box.mk[Bogus = Int64](5)` loads clean because the guard swallows the whole list when the callee has no OP-level params. One more WI-708-style seeding rung; without it §5.3's construction-site selection means nothing. (2) BRACKET KEY -> SLOT resolution, in the §4.2 order: (1) a declared TYPE PARAMETER name — the operation's, or its enclosing sort's — matched by SYMBOL IDENTITY (`n == name_sym`, typing.rs:12931), because both sides are the bare spelling (`build_call_type_args` lowers the key to a bare interned Symbol, \"the param label (A) … NOT a caller-scope value\"; op.type_params holds `kb.intern(\"T\")`, WI-708); (2) a requirement's SPEC SHORT NAME when unambiguous among the callee's remaining ANONYMOUS slots — this one CANNOT match by identity (its candidate set is derived from the slots' canonical, qualified spec symbols), so it is a NAME LOOKUP: `same_label`'s sanctioned family 2 (typing.rs:11328), and THE \"UNAMBIGUOUS AMONG THE REMAINING SLOTS\" CLAUSE IS THE GATE THAT MAKES IT SOUND, not a courtesy — without a gate this is the short-name identity comparison WI-672 deleted (owners `same_sort_canonical` / `same_qname`, neither of which matches on last segment). Anything else is a LOUD error, including on a callee with no type parameters (the phase-0 drop). Rule (2) also admits the DISPATCHED SPEC's OWN short name, so a direct spec-op call selects the dictionary backing it: `Desc.describe[Desc = LoudDesc](w)` — the thing selected is `requirements[0]`. A QUALIFIED key is REFUSED, not resolved (`fold[algebra.Monoid = AddM]`): rule (1) already refuses one (measured — a qualified spelling of a REAL type param is `unknown type-param 'Id.T'`), a resolved key would be a second name law in one bracket list (rule (1)'s key is unresolvable IN PRINCIPLE — `T` is a binder of the callee, visible in no scope — so a ladder mixing them would rank a label against a reference, and resolve-then-fall-back-to-label is a fallback), it would make selection depend on the CALLER's imports (measured: the supply path takes no scope at all — `build_concrete_dispatch_dict`, typing.rs:11700, reads provider facts and resolved symbols only; a cross-namespace call into a `requires`-carrying callee from a namespace that never imports the spec loads clean), and it buys no expressiveness (every selection a short name cannot express is written with the §4.7 NAMED SLOT instead, which puts it under rule (1) — rule (1) is the COMPLETE mechanism, rule (2) a gated shorthand). Also mind `same_label`'s own debug_assert (typing.rs:11336), which fires on the partially-qualified pair. (3) WITNESS VALIDATION at the selection site (§4.4): W provides Spec at the call's bindings (a `SortProvidesInfo(sort_ref = W, spec = Spec[…])` whose spec view unifies with the goal) — else a loud error naming BOTH; the slot exists on the callee; and the selection is REFUSED on a call whose dispatch is already value-directed on a CONCRETE carrier (§1.1 — there the VALUE decides and an explicit witness would silently contradict it; refuse, do not prefer). (4) `resolve` STEP 0 (§4.5): if the call site explicitly bound this goal's spec, return `ResolvedTree::leaf { impl: W, type_args }` after check (1); steps 1-4 untouched — a conditional witness still recursively resolves its own `:-` subgoals and those always SEARCH (tier 2). A bracket key must NOT reach sub-resolutions: rule (2)'s candidate set is the CALLEE's slots, and extending it into the resolution tree would make key resolution depend on which witness was pinned (pinning changes which subgoals exist). Steering a sub-resolution is done by the WITNESS declaring a named slot and the caller binding it in the key's VALUE position (`fold[Monoid = ListM[O = MyEq]]`) — an ordinary type application, where selection happens at the witness's own boundary as a callee slot again. THREADING NEEDS NOTHING NEW (§2.3, §4.6): the resolved instance already rides as a `Value::Requirement` dictionary rebuilt from `sub_requires` at every `apply_within`; the whole change is WHERE the impl in `ResolvedTree::leaf` comes from. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 2, as amended by the second review): `Box.mk[T = String](5)` fails at the ARGUMENT, not the return (§2.2 row 5 flipped); with the carrier's SOLE provider, `fold[Monoid = AddM]([2,3,4])` computes 9 AND THE PIN IS OBSERVABLE — a `[Monoid = NotAProvider]` or wrong-spec witness at the same site is a loud §4.4 error, which is what distinguishes tier 1 from tier 2 silently finding the same provider; a QUALIFIED key, a short name ambiguous across two anonymous slots, and an op type param colliding with its enclosing sort's are each a LOUD error naming the slots. NOTE — the two-monoid VALUE test (9 beside 24) belongs to PHASE 3b, not here: AddM and MulM cannot coexist until tier 3 moves, so as ONE program it does not load at phase 2, and as TWO single-provider programs it measures nothing selection-specific. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §4.2, §4.4, §4.5, §2.3, §9 phase 2."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-839", "WI-840"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-29T16:49:14Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-841", name: "modinst")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-841", author: "user", content: "PHASE 1 (WI-840) DELIVERED 2026-07-29 — two measurements phase 2 must not re-derive. (1) THE SLOT POSITION'S REFERENT IS NOT THE FACT ORDER. `kb.named_requirement_slots(owner)` gives (binder, position); the position indexes SOURCE order, which `SortInfo.requires` preserves and the `SortRequiresInfo` FACT order does NOT — `resolve_requires_bindings` (load.rs) RETRACTS and re-asserts every requirement whose op bindings it completes, moving it to the end of the functor index. Measured: a sort declaring `requires O: Ord[T = E]` then a parameterless `requires Marker` comes back `Marker, Ord` as facts while `SortInfo.requires` stays `Ord, Marker`, so a reader that took `direct_requires`' enumeration (facts) for the referent resolves `O` to the WRONG requirement. Pinned by `the_sort_level_slot_position_indexes_sort_info_requires`, which also asserts the divergence so the warning cannot go stale. At OP level the referent is the flattened GOAL order (`op_requires_entries`, value preconditions included), NOT `OperationInfo.requires`, which holds CLAUSES — `convert_clause_list_with_extra` collapses a multi-goal clause into one `conjunction`, so `requires plus: Monoid[T], times: Monoid[T]` indexes a list of length 1 there. IF the mapping turns out load-bearing rather than incidental, the coordinate-free fix is to put the binder ON the requirement (a third `SortRequiresInfo` field, which the retract/re-assert carries) — recorded in the `named_requirement_slots` doc. (2) THE BINDER IS AN ORDINARY TYPE PARAMETER, so rule (1) already reaches it: measured, `biFold[lo = ByValue](n)` loads on `operation biFold(n) requires lo: Ord[T = Int64]` while `biFold[Bogus = …]` is loud. What phase 2 still owes is the SORT-LEVEL seeding rung (§2.2 rows 5-6) and the key->slot resolution; the op-level binder needs no new seeding. (3) THE §4.2 GUARD IS ALREADY IN, at `check_op_type_param_shadowing` (load.rs), covering all four sources incl. the two-scope collision phase 2's ticket lists — so phase 2 inherits it rather than adding it. NOTE a fourth case the ticket does not list, found by driving: written BARE, `requires Ord[…]` beside a `[Ord]` param resolves `Ord` to the PARAMETER (the capture already happened), so there is no spec left to be ambiguous with; it has its own diagnosis.", at: "2026-07-29T10:39:12Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-841", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. All four §9 phase-2 pieces, and the acceptance UNDERSTATED what could be measured.\n\n(1) SORT-LEVEL SEEDING RUNG: the bracket's declared list is now the op's params ++ its enclosing sort's, CONCATENATED rather than laddered because phase 1's declaration-site collision guard makes them disjoint — `Box.mk[T = String](5)` now fails at the ARGUMENT (§2.2 row 5 flipped). It reuses the MEMOIZED `sort_type_params_as_pairs`, whose own doc calls it \"the same shape an operation's own type_params take\", and is kind-gated because `impl_parent_of_op` returns a NAMESPACE for a free op. (2) KEY -> SLOT in the §4.2 order; rung (2) spans the op's `requires`, its sort's, and — for a body-less spec op — the dispatched spec itself. A qualified key never enters rung (2), so `same_label`'s debug_assert stays out of reach. (3) §4.4 validation. (4) `resolve` STEP 0 via `ResolutionScope.selected`, gated on `stack.is_empty()` so it reaches the call's own goal and no sub-goal.\n\nTHE PIN IS OBSERVABLE IN A VALUE, which the ticket assigned to 3b. Two providers whose DISPATCH CARRIERS differ coexist today (a ground `fact Monoid[T = Int64]` beside a parametric `fact Monoid[T = E]`) and both answer the concrete goal, so ONE program computes 5 or 99 by its bracket key alone, with the bracket-less control measuring what `pick_most_specific` picks. AddM-beside-MulM on ONE carrier still belongs to 3b.\n\nFOUR SILENT DROPS FOUND BY DRIVING, all \"the author named a provider and nothing used it\". The decisive one: a witness providing the spec at OTHER bindings failed THREE DIFFERENT WAYS on the three routes a slot can be served by — loud (spec-op dispatch), LOADS CLEAN then `Internal(__req_monoid not bound)` at eval (dictionary build), and SILENTLY IGNORED (value-direction, which has no dictionary channel at all — WI-822 leg 1). Fixed at the CALL SITE, once, because only two of the three have a place to complain from; enumerating consumers is the mistake WI-839's own review had to undo. Also: a pinned dep that failed to project fell back to `Ok(None)` (\"no dict, carry on\"), and the WI-239 defer PRE-CHECK swallowed the pin before any resolution ran — TWO defer gates were needed, not one.\n\n/code-review (4 agents) then found the most valuable defect: MY NEW CHECK WAS VACUOUS ON THE STDLIB'S OWN SPELLING. `goal_from_op_requires_entry` read NAMED args only, but an op-scoped `requires` is a BARE APPLICATION and the stdlib writes it POSITIONALLY (`operation member(x: T, l: List) -> Bool requires Eq[T]`, prelude/list.anthill:58; `requires Ring[F]`). MEASURED: `requires Monoid[T = HT]` refused a wrong-bindings pin while `requires Monoid[HT]` loaded clean. Now binds positionals in declared param order. RECORDED not fixed: `RequiresEntry.spec` has TWO shapes and FOUR readers that disagree about the bare one — `unwrap_spec_view` drops its bindings, which also makes `entries_cover` vacuously cover any same-spec dep.\n\nTWO DOC CLAIMS I WROTE WERE FALSE, both disproved by review. (a) \"a per-sort predicate would give the §1.1 criterion a second implementation to drift from\" — `sort_has_constructors` already existed, and it is a DIFFERENT criterion (SortInfo.constructors vs Entity-kind children), so the fix was to say which one §1.1 means and to probe membership BOTH raw and canonical (the set is raw-keyed). (b) \"the position is still the slot's identity for the projection path\" — nothing on that path reads it; it is recorded-but-unread, and now says so.\n\nCOORDINATE-FREE, per WI-840's own feedback: `NamedRequirementSlot` records the SPEC BESIDE the position, so the typer matches by spec multiset and never maps a coordinate through a list whose order it does not hold. WHICH entry of a same-spec pair is tagged named is arbitrary — sound only because a selection is SPEC-KEYED so it is unobservable and only the COUNT is read; said out loud rather than left as accident.\n\nCLEANUPS APPLIED: one shared slot enumerator for both consumers (they were two walks that had already DRIFTED — only one filtered value preconditions, under a doc comment asserting they could not disagree); one `pinned_witness_for` owner for what was five copies; both loop-invariant guards hoisted to the iteration SOURCE; `selection_witness_sym` reduced to `sort_functor_of_view`; `same_sort_canonical` not raw `==` where two decoders meet; `sorts_with_constructors` hoisted out of the per-binding loop. DECLINED with reason: threading `selected` on `SigmaCtx` — the cache key needs `selected` by CONTENT and `disambig` only by PRESENCE, and it would make coarse-mode-with-selections inexpressible.\n\nNON-VACUITY: fifteen checks reverted in turn, each failing the expected tests (sort-level rung 6; rung 2 11; step-0 filter 2; site binding check 1; op-scoped goal reader 1; positional bindings 1; pinned-dep refusal 1; check 3 1; Strategy-1/2 skip 1; memo key 1 — and that one needed a NEW test, since no existing one made two differently-pinned calls in one program; both defer gates 1 each; both hoisted guards 2). 26 tests. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh (3708 passed, 0 failed); stdlib loads clean through the CLI. Spec amended (kernel-language.md §Operation type parameters: the two rungs, the three rung-(2) refusals, §4.4 validation) and 058 §2.2/§4.2/§4.5/§9 updated with what phase 2 measured. scaland NOT mirrored: it has no typer and no instance resolution (no SortProvidesInfo, no dispatch), so phase 2 has nothing to mirror — the same finding as WI-839.", at: "2026-07-29T16:49:50Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-841", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the delivery note above, found by driving a case I had asserted without running. That note says the pin is validated on all three routes and that the value-directed one diverges only after phase 3b. FALSE. With two providers that coexist today (their dispatch carriers differ), an OP-SCOPED `requires` computed the answer of the provider the author did NOT name: `Holder.probe[Monoid = AddM](2, 3)` = 99, which is AnyM's. An op-scoped chain has no dictionary channel at all (`synth_req_names` is keyed by the parent SORT), so the bracket was accepted, validated, and discarded — a silently wrong number in a loading program.\n\nAn op-scoped selection is now REFUSED whenever two or more providers answer the goal, which is exactly when the ignored selection could pick wrong. With the carrier's SOLE provider the pin necessarily names it, so that case still loads and computes — which is what §9 phase 2's acceptance asks for. The refusal lifts with WI-822 leg 1. The diagnostic does not pretend the workaround is free: moving the `requires` to the sort threads it but imposes the requirement on every dispatch through that sort, which is why `List.member`'s `Eq[T]` is written on the operation.\n\nTWO OF MY OWN TESTS WERE FALSE POSITIVES for the same reason: they pinned the witness the SEARCH already picks (`[Monoid = AnyM]` -> 99) and so passed without anything being selected. Pin the provider the search would NOT choose, or the assertion is vacuous. Both corrected.\n\nSO: PHASE 2 DELIVERS SELECTION ON TWO ROUTES, NOT THREE — the sort-level dictionary build and the spec-op dispatch. The proposal (058 §4.5) and the check's own doc now say so; the earlier claim that a bracket 'selects the provider' was too broad. Full workspace green (3717 passed, 0 failed).", at: "2026-07-29T18:42:29Z")
